    {
      "title": "Alleged spy plot targeting CEO raises questions about Nvidia's future",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/alleged-spy-plot-targeting-ceo-raises-questions-about-nvidia-s-future-126111100013_1.html",
      "description": "Investigations into whether Nvidia's CEO was the target of a \"foreign intelligence effort\" are raising questions about the future of the chipmaker, which became one of the world's most valuable companies by providing technology that powered the artificial intelligence boom.\nJensen Huang has been synonymous with Nvidia since he co-founded it more than 30 years ago. The company now faces potential damage to its reputation and stock price, uncertainty over a possible successor and new scrutiny from shareholders and elected officials.\nPresident Donald Trump said Monday that the FBI is investigating Huang's affair with an alleged Chinese government agent. But Trump said he spoke with the executive and believes Huang \"had absolutely no idea that the person in question was a spy.\"\nWith a valuation of more than USD 4 trillion, the company has outsized importance as a Wall Street behemoth and provider of chips relied upon by AI developers. Law enforcement and members of Congress, as well as",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-11T02:21:08"
    },
    {
      "title": "Nvidia investigates alleged spy plot involving its CEO: What we know",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/nvidia-investigates-alleged-spy-plot-involving-its-ceo-what-we-know-126111001079_1.html",
      "description": "Stunning allegations of a spy plot have prompted investigations of Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's affair with a purported Chinese government agent.\nOver the weekend, Huang admitted to having an extramarital affair, and acknowledged that news reports surrounding the relationship and potential national security concerns led Nvidia's board to investigate. He and his chipmaking company did not name the person he had been intimate with, but The New York Times identified her as business executive and Chinese citizen Alice Huang. The outlet said US intelligence determined she was gathering information for Beijing.\nChina's Foreign Ministry has dismissed the spying allegations, saying on Monday they amount to a smear campaign.\nMany questions remain unanswered. But the scandal is already bringing turmoil to Nvidia, whose high-end chips have emerged as the leading building blocks for artificial intelligence. Huang himself has become a key face in the AI race, both across the industry and often on U",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-11T02:13:00"
    },
